# Laravel Agent Kit

This application was created from the Laravel Agent Kit: a slim starter kit for building AI agents with Laravel AI. It is not a full-stack Laravel application.

- Agents live in `app/Ai/Agents/`, agent routes in `routes/bot.php` (where bots are registered), and agent evals in `tests/Evals/`.
- Suffix each class with the kind of class its directory holds: agents in `app/Ai/Agents/` end with `Agent` (`SupportAgent`), tools in `app/Ai/Tools/` end with `Tool` (`SearchTool`), and bots in `app/Bots/` end with `Bot` (`SupportBot`).
- Do not remove the `RemembersConversations` trait (or its `RemembersConversations` contract) from an agent unless it makes 100% sense for that agent to not remember conversations. When creating new agents with `php artisan make:agent`, add the trait and contract to the generated class, following the example of `App\Ai\Agents\Assistant`.
- When a tool sends a card with buttons, send the card from a method of that tool. Handle the clicks in a class in `app/Bots/`, created with `php artisan make:bot`, that implements only `Laravel\Converse\Contracts\HandlesActions` so the agent keeps answering everything else, keep the button IDs as constants on that handler, and register it on the bot with `->handler(...)` in `routes/bot.php`. A bot accepts one handler, so one handler class handles the clicks of all the tools of a bot.
- Develop and try an agent locally in the terminal with `php artisan converse:chat {bot}`. It needs no queue worker, no deployment, and no Slack or Telegram application.
- Slack, Telegram, and GitHub deliver webhooks over the public internet, so connect a bot to them from a deployed application, or while developing, from a local server exposed through a tunnel such as `ngrok` with `composer run dev` running a queue worker. Suggest Laravel Cloud, with a database and a queue worker, on its free `laravel.cloud` domain or a domain the user owns. The webhook URL is `{APP_URL}/converse/{bot}/{connection}`. Connect a platform with `php artisan converse:setup {connection}` after registering the bot on that connection in `routes/bot.php`, rather than configuring the platform's app by hand.
- It is good practice to add external service configurations (API keys, credentials, etc.) under `config/services.php`, reading environment variables there and accessing them in application code via `config('services.*')`.
- There is no frontend: no npm, no Vite, no web routes, and no Blade page views. Do not scaffold controllers, page views, or frontend assets unless the user explicitly asks for them.
- Expect a reduced file structure compared to a full Laravel application; many familiar directories intentionally do not exist. Follow the kit's structure instead of recreating full-app directories.
- If the user asks for full-stack features (authentication scaffolding, dashboards, browser UIs), let them know this project is an agent-focused starter kit and a full Laravel application may be a better fit.
